{"objects":[{"id":"flow","instance":"instance--bb430336-87b6-5ea2-9c4c-b66c6d016eff","objects":["attack-action--1","attack-action--2","attack-action--3","attack-action--4","attack-action--5","attack-action--6","attack-action--7","attack-action--8","attack-action--9","attack-action--10","attack-action--11","attack-action--12","attack-action--13","attack-action--14","attack-action--15","attack-action--16","attack-action--17","attack-condition--1","attack-condition--2","attack-condition--3","file--fw-report-doc","windows-registry-key--service-wow64","mutex--username-prefixed","file--db3","file--ocx","file--2018-image","file--shld-ocx","windows-registry-key--appx","instance--ad1f125b-7701-5e9d-b869-79f40471648d","instance--384798bd-a84b-5feb-8d38-7f9d0a087f11","instance--ed6039fe-678b-5ae9-9576-73e6ea35b0c8","instance--7e8c9301-c3f8-55e9-8894-e81f1b6203d9","instance--672b0bb2-5c1d-5df3-9126-38f7203f1a69","instance--3622ace1-abf1-5f6e-a5de-a6f584dc1f66","instance--56b40d44-cc57-5e8b-b320-779a685089fd","instance--895aab9a-87c3-51d8-91f8-cc3f6dd1a5a5","instance--3bb881aa-a5ab-51ba-b53d-89d26964ce7d","instance--be99ba49-e121-58a8-ba7b-dd3de316cfcf","instance--57bd700a-faa2-5f72-95f2-2694bcea17c9","instance--c075f5c6-95e8-5553-8f7e-b432b96021c7","instance--3b404379-7d7e-5278-b499-6ac12ee4e90f","instance--3e0f78a3-bb54-55bd-8490-3453d5e06d04","instance--b0a75c9c-327a-5bed-86e5-422240e7dea4","instance--cb7da505-6791-507a-9a93-f9914c292d1b","instance--56da8838-1109-5fce-9d89-7b3ec32d7010","instance--bf009eb2-0ae7-59c6-babb-b958537d42fc","instance--156bc2ce-eb9a-5524-af2b-23d1a262a730","instance--a3447ebb-a01e-500b-b7ce-2af5b05977ad","instance--4251bf5d-3e48-588f-8ca0-cb6a3d1d812b","instance--1af28c87-980a-5cb4-9208-d3c2e5b11e5d","instance--9b474057-180a-55d3-9e57-fef034bf0ec0","instance--87edf09a-a79b-56c0-a92f-0162877844ed","instance--fd206467-44fa-533f-b899-66a2b6e594de","instance--24d19abc-c6c7-5500-a2e0-dd796883306f","instance--d538960a-0ed7-55d4-b129-4dfeb9e9441d"],"properties":[["name","OceanLotus decoy delivery and persistence flow"],["description","ESET researchers detail the latest tricks and techniques OceanLotus uses to deliver its backdoor while staying under the radar"],["scope","campaign"],["author",[["name","ESET Research"]]],["external_references",[["item-1",[["source_name","www.welivesecurity.com"],["url","https://www.welivesecurity.com/2019/03/20/fake-or-fake-keeping-up-with-oceanlotus-decoys/"]]]]]]},{"anchors":{"0":"instance--ad592d05-6969-5b51-98a8-1ad9b6aab896","120":"instance--5850a8f4-8241-5e73-9bf0-dcd376cd5025","150":"instance--0da1498f-3beb-5916-a249-e0410a4a81e4","180":"instance--d3632784-7391-5a29-83e2-edab51db56c6","210":"instance--8bc47ca5-ca73-5a04-a9ea-918608f10871","240":"instance--6546eef6-c5ad-516e-a417-89916e651648","270":"instance--244e5a69-f465-564e-9d2c-93d4da44fc57","30":"instance--6a1fca3c-fdb6-536a-bbea-61ac2349a700","300":"instance--043a5b44-2864-5565-abce-1909204e5485","330":"instance--e10eb918-2b0b-56f4-9c82-f345ce21276c","60":"instance--33a99d1b-dad8-51f6-be74-e1614db9151b","90":"instance--380c79e9-b1eb-547c-bf01-a011db1201c1"},"id":"action","instance":"attack-action--1","properties":[["name","Deliver malicious RTF document"],["description","First stage\n\nThis document FW Report on demonstration of former CNRP in Republic of Korea.doc ( ) is similar to the one mentioned in the article above, and also interesting as it really targets people interested in Cambodian politics (the CNRP \u2013 Cambodia National Rescue Party \u2013 political party was dissolved in late 2017). Despite its .doc extension, the document is actually in RTF format (see Figure 1), contains many garbage groups, and is also malformed."],["confidence",0.0],["ttp",{"tactic":"TA0001","technique":"T1566.001"}]]},{"anchors":{"0":"instance--4f4d03a8-4502-5010-997b-9943e254395b","120":"instance--cefe62ac-5b6e-5ead-84fc-b4c632913a73","150":"instance--4d07a740-2467-5b95-ac8d-4a2ee8edd11e","180":"instance--1205f03c-a457-5a0b-87ef-3bbb7aac110f","210":"instance--96e7dc6a-9f86-5f69-8d45-a566303cd4c0","240":"instance--66237af6-bb19-5a51-9fa2-cbc4a96186f5","270":"instance--836dfd42-b475-50ca-9acf-4f11bd32b89f","30":"instance--efe92997-cc5c-538a-90bc-c09ffb1379f4","300":"instance--9c4a24ae-1426-5cb5-9d7c-792e6dd325b7","330":"instance--06c6a74a-ca7d-5703-88ea-a06a0933d21a","60":"instance--5e4f84e5-56b7-5b8f-9bc3-b4d59d371123","90":"instance--019deb6a-bab3-57db-9a53-11e3564bf7a4"},"id":"action","instance":"attack-action--2","properties":[["name","Exploit Equation Editor vulnerability"],["description","An overflow in the field is possible because its size isn\u2019t checked before being copied. A name that is too long triggers the vulnerability. As seen in the RTF file content (offset 0xC26 in Figure 2), the buffer is filled with shellcode followed by a NOP (0x90) sled and the return address . That address is a gadget in pointing to a instruction. This results in pointing at the beginning of the field which contains the shellcode."],["confidence",0.0],["ttp",{"tactic":"TA0002","technique":"T1203"}]]},{"anchors":{"0":"instance--0ce35aea-35a2-52ae-a67b-985306bdefe7","120":"instance--312639d5-e6d9-57b1-850d-28f0a1eac2f6","150":"instance--9551a25e-2558-512e-9894-dd64a8d662bf","180":"instance--403becce-e0ad-5dcd-bc14-7c00453907da","210":"instance--2305f501-2712-5ec6-9d4e-1371b011e937","240":"instance--cf1b8cf6-1cbd-5afb-b653-aa693889dab5","270":"instance--1d3dc59a-40a0-5670-aa3e-1304ff762003","30":"instance--77063d4f-503a-5e9b-b622-15aa2cc6a8e7","300":"instance--beaab67f-8a54-5982-8c5d-9d2ed2d42c9f","330":"instance--4d3274ff-ef14-5a53-99be-01575194a98e","60":"instance--4154a6bf-290b-5fce-a557-5dc0f3e700fb","90":"instance--2992bc0b-df57-54e5-a17b-03a964338c09"},"id":"action","instance":"attack-action--3","properties":[["name","Locate open document handle and extract next shellcode"],["description","The purpose of the shellcode is to execute a second piece of shellcode, embedded inside the open document. First, the initial shellcode tries to find the handle of the open document file by iterating through all the system\u2019s handles ( with the argument) and checking if the handle\u2019s matches the of a process and if the document was opened with the following access mask: . To confirm it found the right handle and not the handle of another open document, the content of the file is mapped with the function and the shellcode checks if the last four bytes of the document are \" \"; this technique is called \u201cEgg Hunting\u201d. Once it finds a match, the document is copied to a temporary folder ( ) as . Then the last 12 bytes of the document are read."],["confidence",0.0],["ttp",{"tactic":"TA0005","technique":"T1027"}]]},{"anchors":{"0":"instance--0f6e4da7-a5f4-54d7-866f-3bc6e8bcb019","120":"instance--015254b5-0906-577e-b3e3-3899f837d118","150":"instance--f7af5b8c-330a-58d5-b0da-a3d5f0bc6ad0","180":"instance--9f27ab21-2382-567d-b022-74ad5a29bd08","210":"instance--d799eb0a-7600-5b1a-a298-c47a78ceae4b","240":"instance--911c2c52-5974-5e08-b0b6-a9084d57c6ee","270":"instance--286444b4-97a1-58cd-9ca3-cffa04a15351","30":"instance--92192c38-5143-50e0-8c39-a96ea86e32e2","300":"instance--82bb3de0-bea5-5651-a1de-583283258c6d","330":"instance--4f0945c8-f644-50ce-8796-fea52a29dda1","60":"instance--16c43a2d-7356-521d-a507-5922e2c3337b","90":"instance--abce5c62-339a-5ed5-a0b8-dd66136cfe2f"},"id":"action","instance":"attack-action--4","properties":[["name","Invoke extracted shellcode"],["description","The 32-bit value between the and markers is the offset to the next shellcode. It is invoked using the function. The extracted shellcode is the same that the OceanLotus group has been using for a while now."],["confidence",0.0],["ttp",{"tactic":"TA0005","technique":"T1055"}]]},{"anchors":{"0":"instance--a469636c-0cb2-50b9-92f9-644c8aa98197","120":"instance--d57b3a21-e910-5879-8148-40165cea451a","150":"instance--58250ef4-5bb4-5bf3-b33e-89493bbb395d","180":"instance--3e769cf2-c1e5-5d2e-81d4-892f48ce4d86","210":"instance--f6499e41-749c-5d7f-9346-96c08d0d5dfd","240":"instance--d9dcdfc8-5f42-546a-803a-0efcd5d4cf91","270":"instance--c9afd0f6-97ce-57b2-9d60-3557b6214809","30":"instance--75f82155-f5ff-5619-8bf8-aae22937cab0","300":"instance--25e2d26e-1b35-56bd-b9aa-096c2d3b0e3c","330":"instance--e0483688-da10-5376-8cf2-050bcc3b70f0","60":"instance--1293d9cc-16b2-5c67-a453-bd144aab133a","90":"instance--2b43cd52-10db-5b15-8ad5-c4b59496528b"},"id":"action","instance":"attack-action--5","properties":[["name","Drop components from stage resource"],["description","The stage\u2019s resource is parsed and the files are dropped in either or in the randomly chosen folder. The creation times are changed to have the same values as .\n\nFor example, here is a folder and a list of files created by picking the executable as a source of data."],["confidence",0.0],["ttp",{"tactic":"TA0005","technique":"T1036.005"}]]},{"anchors":{"0":"instance--54fc1f38-f4e3-523e-b07c-73889b0d8df7","120":"instance--9cf45948-7040-5d7a-a1d5-3cb4db0c1af6","150":"instance--13bb13cc-e02b-51d2-8d7d-40e5613b6e5a","180":"instance--e33bc6ee-771e-54fe-a772-74dfdaad420d","210":"instance--286f51a3-8fc6-55ec-978e-8b05ebda3655","240":"instance--14659696-be77-5460-beb1-4b5eac126e43","270":"instance--f48ed1a6-2895-5cd2-9b1c-e30ac2bc8921","30":"instance--7b803e61-16ac-5720-92cd-8f2a456a52b7","300":"instance--76c24361-5143-5c43-8e4d-707c619e6038","330":"instance--f395cfc7-395a-5001-9552-a4fec6dbc53a","60":"instance--760885bb-9294-5a5b-93a2-a410e1ea4112","90":"instance--8e0d1a12-8611-598d-9a7d-40f2525e4781"},"id":"action","instance":"attack-action--6","properties":[["name","Create persistence service"],["description","The service name is the filename without extension; the display name is the folder name but if it already exists then the string \u201c \u201d is appended (the number is incremented until it finds an unused name). The operators made sure the persistence through the service would be resilient: on service failure, the service should restart after 1 second. Then, the registry value WOW64 of the new service key is set to 4 which indicates that it\u2019s a 32-bit service."],["confidence",0.0],["ttp",{"tactic":"TA0003","technique":"T1543.003"}]]},{"anchors":{"0":"instance--7cccbcd0-a724-54fb-be4f-ac523d26f7d0","120":"instance--738e44b2-1357-5eb3-82bd-8a0ce8942b72","150":"instance--d31f4735-6339-5929-8d7c-d30863cf166f","180":"instance--2252393d-d801-5931-ad38-4b53374a9880","210":"instance--065b5ad5-023a-5336-b458-e9c9dcda2976","240":"instance--5ea0dd2b-5eac-51f8-855a-4cbcb801bc2d","270":"instance--bba66874-a069-56c6-bb95-967cb3cefc9e","30":"instance--6377c09d-ecb4-5567-a2f4-4dde56ff4f98","300":"instance--ede3430f-b16b-58ae-be71-1d1f1a194336","330":"instance--7772f482-8ee2-569a-b600-7528c065d4e0","60":"instance--98139a98-16b0-54fe-8c77-3473c1b520cd","90":"instance--5cb9cc95-e190-5a68-985d-0a94d39299e6"},"id":"action","instance":"attack-action--7","properties":[["name","Create hidden scheduled task"],["description","The scheduled task is created via several COM interfaces: , , , and . Essentially, the malware creates a hidden task, sets the account information with the current user or the administrator information and sets the trigger. This is a daily task with a duration of 24 hours and the interval between two executions is set to 10 minutes, which means it will run all the time."],["confidence",0.0],["ttp",{"tactic":"TA0003","technique":"T1053.005"}]]},{"anchors":{"0":"instance--5150f7cc-6f25-5eb3-a6fc-5099ced4caf4","120":"instance--64a408ce-c543-55d6-a9dd-206ce7cadbb2","150":"instance--1b71e413-cfd2-528c-8792-dc7dd1bb9938","180":"instance--aa12ce11-0165-52b6-b72d-e4d9070e9fbb","210":"instance--5d8b0f08-14bc-58f4-9316-b25a92dd09a6","240":"instance--34f96b84-9453-53f2-ae07-d28c04665e6c","270":"instance--415f1d54-1cd4-5ba7-8071-4aa99bc946ac","30":"instance--18834cac-b4c4-57b0-88ea-5a62fdb27cf9","300":"instance--de4f9957-1ac6-5782-86ea-f1a3b6a9ad13","330":"instance--8eff940b-86bb-556f-8c85-c7c47577b3ed","60":"instance--14a123fd-5626-5e69-8257-6dcd64c28ec0","90":"instance--02294ff1-7261-5db8-8e0d-b9e818922053"},"id":"action","instance":"attack-action--8","properties":[["name","Side-load malicious DLL"],["description","In our example, the executable ( ) is legitimate software side-loading the DLLs that were dropped with it. In this case, the is the interesting one."],["confidence",0.0],["ttp",{"tactic":"TA0005","technique":"T1574.002"}]]},{"anchors":{"0":"instance--180f2983-9c1a-56f4-99d6-c990125f7baa","120":"instance--c7f65318-7600-57f4-a8ac-992357e695ff","150":"instance--95523e1a-0a1d-52ef-944c-23f9cbc50233","180":"instance--bc8eb48d-411d-5877-aa9b-966476b71207","210":"instance--4cc5026e-07f9-5327-9912-5d278a0a3321","240":"instance--d6a7d51d-39a4-5eb3-8831-f23437781b95","270":"instance--cac30e05-5f01-5b4f-b5c0-6b975297df2d","30":"instance--cb0751b0-c027-5895-a460-a72e48920c71","300":"instance--f41cdc1f-abb7-5ff7-81c2-bf2235a7faab","330":"instance--b114cbda-9ec8-5612-b2f8-93c36811cafb","60":"instance--f28e5296-2041-5fe9-992d-4c70d549191e","90":"instance--3ea39244-da0f-5435-8745-f2afa8b13872"},"id":"action","instance":"attack-action--9","properties":[["name","Patch legitimate code section and redirect execution"],["description","After these deceptive checks, the code gets the section of , changes its protection to and overwrites it with do-nothing instructions that have no side effects:\n\nFigure 8 -- Sequence of instructions without side effects\n\nAt the end, a instruction to the address of the function :: exported by is appended. This means that, after loading the malicious DLL, when the runtime calls in , the instruction pointer will point to the NOP sled, which will eventually call , the next stage."],["confidence",0.0],["ttp",{"tactic":"TA0005","technique":"T1055"}]]},{"anchors":{"0":"instance--db5fab04-54d4-5eec-a429-185861eb2719","120":"instance--985391bd-d017-5935-ab31-3b4b56331b02","150":"instance--092bb98d-fc30-5f69-b6b7-e3024f3ec65a","180":"instance--755129e6-612e-5b63-a780-1c886d98909b","210":"instance--431723d9-dd8c-5190-9dab-6f346173c044","240":"instance--5b9fb821-54fe-5f22-bba3-038263245910","270":"instance--3c6777cb-6430-544b-b31a-01b198524553","30":"instance--c070071b-a9a3-52fe-8956-a51744db200b","300":"instance--a9a4289c-d995-5783-9544-a8b45052eb56","330":"instance--344009cc-e024-5c68-9246-51e85e00d45c","60":"instance--86c77621-2c1d-59c3-b398-e62f4b48a3dd","90":"instance--67627b08-1913-5f3d-8318-52d3bda9c1c3"},"id":"action","instance":"attack-action--10","properties":[["name","Create user mutex and execute db3 shellcode"],["description","This function simply creates a mutex starting with and followed by the current username. Then, it reads the dropped file with the \u00a0.db3\u00a0 extension, which contains position-independent code, and uses to execute its content."],["confidence",0.0],["ttp",{"tactic":"TA0005","technique":"T1055"}]]},{"anchors":{"0":"instance--6b7baffd-afaf-5472-9af2-a263119a77ef","120":"instance--83f38e63-e29a-53f4-99d9-927a195c453f","150":"instance--3e720145-b7a1-5bd6-8a7d-bb13519675d1","180":"instance--bdce060f-d96c-5b06-b8f4-1ab1527fd01d","210":"instance--a4feb251-e6e3-5680-a712-d59c92f73ccf","240":"instance--9a564ca6-b753-5b06-880c-b3aac25c1e73","270":"instance--76861a89-f006-5e0d-8c5b-ebdcb5e926dc","30":"instance--cf41e64d-4a4b-58d9-9962-72d278b2c489","300":"instance--ac1d6edd-482f-5f13-9348-b605afcc260c","330":"instance--ed78dfa1-f300-564f-b2cd-6c99552b9a68","60":"instance--979accb3-1b3e-5af4-9182-78392cbaf904","90":"instance--ae721c42-320e-5ea4-9c77-339a05b6e12f"},"id":"action","instance":"attack-action--11","properties":[["name","Extract final backdoor"],["description","The script extracts the final stage. This component is the backdoor that we already analysed in this white paper: . It is recognizable as such from the GUID that is present in the binary. The configuration of the malware is still encrypted in a PE resource."],["confidence",0.0],["ttp",{"tactic":"TA0005","technique":"T1027.009"}]]},{"anchors":{"0":"instance--f1e35721-7ac2-54fe-bcf6-5456d2faeaa3","120":"instance--3ff6777e-fcba-5b31-bf0f-3ddf77f6030d","150":"instance--cc6192f9-21a5-5d1a-b954-6a52d989b82d","180":"instance--62a87260-0181-5f49-bc05-733216e4d822","210":"instance--2f2d05c5-8e60-5ac2-b7af-e03027d67957","240":"instance--79c1ec18-d792-5d3d-a7bc-d94fe8b124cf","270":"instance--a035d357-e930-5d33-ab8d-d0314092d036","30":"instance--3af551b8-f6ac-5f54-9f6d-403fecdc69eb","300":"instance--298b5d2a-bf93-51e9-b052-19ce36114a5d","330":"instance--c8ea0da0-a19a-5766-b0d1-8524844a59d1","60":"instance--a204f87d-ace7-5a9e-b3d1-13202bdc5c7b","90":"instance--fff4de11-3f84-52c8-bb77-192210612a62"},"id":"action","instance":"attack-action--12","properties":[["name","Run SFX archive and drop OCX and decoy image"],["description","When run, these self-extracting RAR files drop and execute DLL files (with a .ocx extension) with the final payload being the previously documented . . Since the middle of January 2019, OceanLotus began reusing the technique but changed some configuration over time.\n\nThe malware drops (SHA-1: ) as well as the image 2018 ."],["confidence",0.0],["ttp",{"tactic":"TA0002","technique":"T1204.002"}]]},{"anchors":{"0":"instance--cac05670-e90e-512c-99cc-c0adf909c3ef","120":"instance--43922820-ddd3-5742-a88d-49c20f8e3fd5","150":"instance--884271a6-8d7b-5c96-9ece-61be4126898b","180":"instance--373a94ef-653d-5c24-96f7-d0cf326d3aea","210":"instance--e58b782a-e75e-5141-bdf0-6d47dbed96bd","240":"instance--5960e46a-3307-5101-b96f-f1bae10e323d","270":"instance--a5ae1125-6ca5-54f1-b9d3-0091a0a38367","30":"instance--e7c521dd-fe09-528b-bb67-1ec53389b876","300":"instance--582e0864-cb5c-5de1-b6c0-e162ae91e8bc","330":"instance--3d5550f3-3e33-5d71-b71e-db2a0a957e5e","60":"instance--b83fc757-0bd4-5b63-a13e-e5b8ef5956d2","90":"instance--2a745819-837b-5585-a366-81a34117a6e5"},"id":"action","instance":"attack-action--13","properties":[["name","Invoke OCX twice via SFX script"],["description","You may have noticed the first two lines in the SFX script invoke the OCX file twice, but it is not a mistake...\n\n{9ec60ada-a200-4159-b310-8071892ed0c3}.ocx (ShLd.dll)"],["confidence",0.0],["ttp",{"tactic":"TA0002","technique":"T1218.010"}]]},{"anchors":{"0":"instance--2accaaf8-d071-542e-9a10-06866a96b394","120":"instance--75ef5b2f-7c71-566e-baf2-29f54ced8138","150":"instance--73017f0a-a5f3-5dca-b91c-a2d37bcfe21b","180":"instance--2f1b6e5f-d3b0-59e5-b3c8-6c1399d1ce2a","210":"instance--c7aedd91-3346-5b75-9e3a-842851881a25","240":"instance--2146989b-d8a2-53f0-8a1c-cd3d976227e9","270":"instance--1b0bbd91-fa2a-5897-96d0-9e5918961c9c","30":"instance--e6b73353-364e-5586-a07b-e1fdc92ad2d3","300":"instance--0fdc5473-d5b9-5fed-91a3-21958da6287b","330":"instance--fd1cd9e0-5761-5725-801f-bf88b65914e8","60":"instance--a58b8357-6abc-58d7-ad2b-bec48fffcd9b","90":"instance--e8daf33f-0240-5544-9f99-3be7ac96fc1d"},"id":"action","instance":"attack-action--14","properties":[["name","Use registry value as encoded function offset"],["description","Basically, the first time the is called, it sets the registry value to an encoded offset in the DLL ( ). The second time the function is called, it reads this very same value and executes the function at that address. From there, the resource is read and executed and many in-memory operations are executed."],["confidence",0.0],["ttp",{"tactic":"TA0005","technique":"T1112"}]]},{"anchors":{"0":"instance--c80ca3ef-db0a-5372-968e-c17eb31a79ca","120":"instance--69989f45-927f-57be-92e5-5102fccd9f11","150":"instance--9b02c549-b183-5406-a100-249fb1868d16","180":"instance--c1d2a890-ed8d-5123-92d5-ceeaa937ee87","210":"instance--b785b7b9-02d5-5d04-be0c-0d02d4f85620","240":"instance--cde2d3fb-a23f-5188-9d7d-6c7f3cd3cebb","270":"instance--3fa59d94-25bb-5c51-af21-1a7c1fd91f66","30":"instance--fdd04191-1a03-5134-b88e-a55240471931","300":"instance--dddeb587-c160-5bdc-a385-f5d294f11e92","330":"instance--a13b6b46-2b8e-